{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794355306977,"sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","part":{"id":"prt_1c7e920ea0013DfyQDaM7G9CIY","messageID":"msg_1c7e915be001CBlWSIkIAVB1Pd","sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794355306978,"sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","part":{"id":"prt_1c7e920f400165JOwuIlV00X9O","messageID":"msg_1c7e915be001CBlWSIkIAVB1Pd","sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","type":"text","text":"<think>Let me look at the frontend structure.\n\nAlso, note the requirements.lock has werkzeug pinned but the requirements.txt doesn't list it directly (it's a transitive dep of flask). Note that `hashlib` / `werkzeug.security` password hashing is available via werkzeug. Flask's `generate_password_hash` uses pbkdf2 by default in werkzeug 3.x (scrypt requires... actually werkzeug 3.0 default is \"scrypt\"). Werkzeug 3.1's default password hash method is \"scrypt\". That's fine — it's built into werkzeug, no extra deps.\n\nLet me check the frontend.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794355306740,"end":1794355306970}}}
