{"type":"text","timestamp":1794355421687,"sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","part":{"id":"prt_1c7eae1e8001TuHhdRnjtYrQur","messageID":"msg_1c7eaae4e001zD4Fj1EAHZ07mh","sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","type":"text","text":"<think>The constraints file has hashes, so pip requires hashes for all requirements. The requirements.txt lacks hashes, hence the error. But the Dockerfile does the same thing... `pip install -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock` — with a constraints file containing hashes, pip requires hashes for all packages. Hmm, but the Dockerfile presumably works (the base project builds). Wait — does it? The constraint file entries have `--hash=`, which forces require-hashes mode. And requirements.txt has no hashes → error.\n\nHmm, but the README says the image is built from the Dockerfile, so the base project must build successfully. Let me test the exact Dockerfile pip command in the venv to see.\n\nActually pip's behavior: if any requirement (including constraints?) has a hash, pip switches to require-hashes mode for the whole resolution. Constraints files with hashes do trigger this. Let me check by running exactly the Dockerfile command.\n\nActually the error above says exactly that: \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements... flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4b...\" — meaning it wants the hash for flask which is in the lock but the main requirement lacks it.\n\nSo the Docker build with these files would fail?! Unless pip behaves differently in the Docker context (no difference). Hmm, that suggests the base project image doesn't actually build... but the task states the app is built from the Dockerfile. Maybe pip's behavior differs by version: the Dockerfile's python:3.13-slim has a specific pip version. Newer pip (25.x) enforces require-hashes when constraints contain hashes? Let me test with the exact same command in my venv: `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock` — that's what I ran (with --quiet). Same thing.\n\nLet me check pip's actual behavior more carefully: In pip, `--require-hashes` is implied if any requirement in the *requirements file* has a hash. Constraints with hashes... I recall pip's `RequirementSet` collects hashes from constraints too, causing this error in newer pip versions.\n\nThe fix: add hashes to requirements.txt, or make requirements.txt include the hashes. The cleanest minimal change: put the hashes in requirements.txt lines. Or remove the hashes from the lock's constraint entries.\n\nSince the README says \"Dependencies are pinned: backend/requirements.lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file. ... When you change dependencies, update the lock file as well (or remove it).\"\n\nSince I'm not changing dependencies, the lock stays. But the build must work. Let me test what happens in the Docker build environment... I can't run docker. But I can test with the same pip version as python:3.13-slim would have (pip 25.x). My venv's pip: let me check version.\n\nThe safest approach: make requirements.txt carry the hashes too, so require-hashes mode works. But modifying requirements.txt to include hashes for flask and gunicorn means the lock's constraints would conflict? No — constraints just pin versions; hashes in requirements.txt satisfy the hash requirement.\n\nLet me look at how pip treats: requirements.txt entries with `--hash=` → require-hashes mode; then all requirements must have hashes. The constraints file also lists hashes for its entries — consistent.\n\nSo I'll update requirements.txt:\n\n```\nflask==3.1.3 \\\n    --hash=sha256:0ef0e52a8c9d932855379197dd8f94047b359ca0a78695144304cb45f87c9eb \\\n    --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\ngunicorn==26.2.0 \\\n    --hash=sha256:62b864895d9ebff0b2f9867ba04fe811c93121596540830c9c916d0769668447 \\\n    --hash=sha256:bd249d0b3b7972f7432f0a6b6ff7b3ee2d129f70cd1ff6c09a9dd5e29a2b88e3\n```\n\nThat matches the lock's hashes for flask and gunicorn. Then pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock should work.\n\nAlternatively, I could remove the lock file (README says \"or remove it\") — but the Dockerfile prefers the lock when present. Keeping the lock with pinned transitive deps is better.\n\nLet me first verify that adding hashes to requirements.txt makes the install work.\n\nActually wait. Let me double check whether the docker build currently succeeds. The base image python:3.13-slim@... has pip preinstalled (version depends on the image build date; the digest is from ~2025?). Let me check pip's behavior history: the \"require-hashes implied by constraints\" behavior was introduced in pip 25.0? There was a change: \"pip 25.0: Constraints files with hashes now trigger --require-hashes\"? Hmm.\n\nI recall pip's behavior: `--require-hashes` is enabled if any requirement *or constraint* has a hash. This has been the case for a while (pip's `RequirementSet.has_hashes` / `_check_requirements`). Actually, in pip's code: `self.require_hashes` is set in `parse_requirements` for each file; `RequirementSet.require_hashes` property returns `self._require_hashes` set when any requirement has hash options. Constraints are parsed into the same RequirementSet... In older pip versions, constraints were parsed with `parse_requirements` and their hashes were added to the set → triggering require-hashes.\n\nGiven the error we got with pip 25.1.1 locally, and the docker image would likely have pip 25.x too, the build would fail. Since the task says the app is built from the Dockerfile and must work, I should fix this. Adding hashes to requirements.txt is the minimal, safe fix (and matches pip-compile's standard output format, which puts hashes in the requirements file itself).\n\nActually, the standard pip-compile layout: `requirements.txt` (pinned with hashes, the \"locked\" file) and `requirements.in`. Here the repo has requirements.txt (unhashed, direct deps) and requirements.lock (hashed, full transitive). pip-compile output would be the lock file with hashes.\n\nThe cleanest approach that preserves the repo's structure: keep requirements.lock as the hashed full set, and make requirements.txt identical-ish? No.\n\nOption A: Add hashes to requirements.txt for flask and gunicorn. Then `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` works in require-hashes mode (all requirements have hashes; constraints also have hashes). \n\nLet me verify Option A works locally.\n\nAlso I could add `--require-hashes` explicitly? No, that would require hashes for everything including transitive deps from constraints — constraints have hashes so it works. But not needed.\n\nLet me test Option A.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794355421672,"end":1794355421683}}}
