{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794355514745,"sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","part":{"id":"prt_1c7ec4d46001nwN23K1wVxoF1q","messageID":"msg_1c7ec26dd001jASfm4hKIEtEA3","sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794355514746,"sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","part":{"id":"prt_1c7ec4d49001czgJYB598Ms4ie","messageID":"msg_1c7ec26dd001jASfm4hKIEtEA3","sessionID":"ses_e38170798fferFf1mK07jkjW4e","type":"text","text":"<think>Tailwind installed. Note that package.json now has \"^4.3.5\" ranges — the repo pins exact versions. Let me pin exact versions to match repo style (no ^). Also the package-lock.json was updated.\n\nLet me edit package.json to use exact versions: \"tailwindcss\": \"4.3.5\", \"@tailwindcss/vite\": \"4.3.5\". Then re-run npm install to sync the lock.\n\nNow let's write the frontend. Structure:\n\nfrontend/\n  index.html (update title)\n  src/\n    main.tsx\n    index.css (tailwind entry)\n    api.ts\n    types.ts\n    auth.tsx\n    router.tsx\n    components/ (Layout, SeverityBadge, StatusBadge, Field, ErrorMessage...)\n    pages/PublicStatsPage.tsx\n    pages/LoginPage.tsx\n    pages/RegisterPage.tsx\n    pages/IncidentListPage.tsx\n    pages/IncidentDetailPage.tsx\n    pages/NewIncidentPage.tsx\n    App.tsx\n\nLet me design the UI with Tailwind: dark-ish ops dashboard. Keep it clean and professional.\n\nKey details:\n- Token stored in localStorage under \"incident_center_token\" and username under \"incident_center_username\".\n- Auth context provides {token, username, login, register, logout, ready}.\n- API client attaches Authorization header when token present.\n\nLet me write api.ts:\n\n```ts\nexport type Severity = \"sev1\" | \"sev2\" | \"sev3\" | \"sev4\";\nexport type IncidentStatus = \"investigating\" | \"identified\" | \"monitoring\" | \"resolved\" | \"open\" | \"closed\";\n\nexport interface Incident {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  service: string;\n  severity: Severity;\n  status: IncidentStatus;\n  commander: string;\n  impact_summary: string;\n  opened_at: string;\n  resolved_at: string | null;\n  reopen_count: number;\n  opened_by: string;\n  sla_due_at: string;\n  sla_breached: boolean;\n}\n\nexport interface TimelineEvent {\n  id: number;\n  type: string;\n  message: string;\n  created_at: string;\n  author: string;\n}\n\nexport interface IncidentPage { items: Incident[]; total: number; page: number; page_size: number; }\nexport interface PublicStats { open_total: number; open_by_severity: Record<Severity, number>; sla_breached_open: number; resolved_last_30_days: number; }\n```\n\nError handling: an ApiError class with status and errors map. The fetch helper:\n\n```ts\nasync function request<T>(path: string, options: {method?: string; body?: unknown; auth?: boolean} = {}): Promise<T> {\n  const headers: Record<string,string> = {};\n  if (options.body !== undefined) headers[\"Content-Type\"] = \"application/json\";\n  const token = getToken();\n  if (token) headers[\"Authorization\"] = `Bearer ${token}`;\n  const response = await fetch(path, {method: options.method ?? \"GET\", headers, body: options.body !== undefined ? JSON.stringify(options.body) : undefined});\n  const text = await response.text();\n  let payload: any = null;\n  try { payload = text ? JSON.parse(text) : null; } catch { payload = null; }\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    const message = payload?.error ?? `Request failed (${response.status})`;\n    const errors = payload?.errors;\n    throw new ApiError(message, response.status, errors);\n  }\n  return payload as T;\n}\n```\n\nRouter: hash-based.\n\n```tsx\nexport function useHashRoute(): string {\n  const [path, setPath] = useState(() => currentPath());\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const onChange = () => setPath(currentPath());\n    window.addEventListener(\"hashchange\", onChange);\n    return () => window.removeEventListener(\"hashchange\", onChange);\n  }, []);\n  return path;\n}\nexport function navigate(path: string) { window.location.hash = path.startsWith(\"#\") ? path : `#${path}`; }\n```\n\nRoute parsing in App.tsx:\n\n```tsx\nconst path = useHashRoute();\nconst segments = path.split(\"/\").filter(Boolean);\n```\n\nRoutes:\n- [] → PublicPage\n- [\"login\"] → LoginPage\n- [\"register\"] → RegisterPage\n- [\"incidents\"] → IncidentListPage\n- [\"incidents\",\"new\"] → NewIncidentPage\n- [\"incidents\", id] → IncidentDetailPage\n\nProtected routes: if no token → show a \"sign in required\" panel with a link to sign in (and redirect?). I'll redirect to /login when visiting protected pages without a token. Let me implement a `RequireAuth` wrapper that renders a message + link instead of redirecting (safer for UX). Actually redirecting to login is nicer. I'll show a panel with \"Sign in to view incidents\" plus a button that navigates to login. Hmm, the requirement \"Registration and sign-in forms\" must be reachable. I'll do a redirect to `#/login?next=...`? Simpler: show a panel.\n\nLet me do: RequireAuth renders children if token, else a centered card \"You need to sign in\" with a button linking to /login.\n\nNow the pages.\n\n**PublicStatsPage**: \n- Title \"Public status\"\n- Cards: Open incidents (open_total), Past target (sla_breached_open), Resolved last 30 days.\n- Per severity counts: sev1..sev4 with labels.\n- Note: \"aggregate figures only\" — no incident lists.\n- Refresh button + auto refresh maybe.\n\n**LoginPage / RegisterPage**: forms with username/password; error display; on success navigate to /incidents.\n\n**IncidentListPage**:\n- Filters: severity select (all/sev1..sev4), status select (all/investigating/identified/monitoring/resolved), open-only switch (checkbox styled as a switch), search input (debounced or on submit).\n- List: cards with title (link), service, severity badge, status badge, opened time, SLA due time, breached flag \"Past target\".\n- Paging: prev/next buttons + \"Page X of Y\" + total count.\n- \"Open incident\" button → /incidents/new.\n\n**IncidentDetailPage**:\n- Fetch incident + timeline.\n- Show all fields: id, title, service, severity, status, commander, impact summary, opened at, resolved at, reopen count, opened by, sla due, sla breached.\n- Status buttons: one per status; clicking PATCHes status; active one highlighted; the current one disabled maybe.\n- Timeline: list of events oldest first with type badge, author, time, message.\n- Form: textarea + submit → POST timeline event; refresh timeline after.\n\n**NewIncidentPage**:\n- Fields: title (required), service, severity (select, required), commander, impact summary.\n- Submit → POST /api/incidents; on 400 → show per-field validation messages from `errors` plus the top-level error.\n- On success → navigate to the incident detail page.\n\nLet me write the code now. I'll write reasonably compact but complete components.\n\nStyling: Tailwind utility classes; dark theme (slate-950 bg). Let me use a light theme? For an ops team, dark is nice. I'll go with a dark slate theme.\n\nLet me write the files.\n\nAlso update index.html title to \"Incident Center\".\n\nLet me write index.css:\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n```\nThat's the only hand-written CSS (Tailwind's own entry file) — allowed.\n\nvite.config.ts: add tailwindcss plugin.\n\nLet me writeeverything now.</think>\n\nNow the frontend. Let me set up Tailwind and write the app:","time":{"start":1794355514697,"end":1794355514715}}}
